#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:679
#: 5910dda46f734b03af3fcf3d9ce27ffa
msgid "When evaluating if this radio meets your local regulations it may be helpful to know what technology it uses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:682
#: 590610d14780434f89b24df1f7e48024
msgid "The firmware implements frequency hopping spread spectrum (FHSS) with synchronous adaptive time division multiplexing (TDM)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:685
#: e44b450fddba4a9383b932fc8231064b
msgid "Specifically, the radio divides up the frequency range between MIN_FREQ+delta and MAX_FREQ-delta into ``NUM_CHANNELS`` channels. The 'delta' value is a guard range to ensure that we stay well away from the edges of the allowed band. The guard range is set to half a channel width. The channel width is defined as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:695
#: 97caa8567426431e9b1a21e8f033774c
msgid "Additionally, the radio skews the base frequency by up to one channel using a random seed based on ``NETID``. This means that two radios using different ``NETID`` numbers use slightly different frequencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:699
#: 81153752986540f69b442c2abffb408a
msgid "The radios use GFSK (Gaussian Frequency Shift Keying) for transmission on a particular frequency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:702
#: 4aeb0cd300e24bbe92b63aad07d4c4ac
msgid "The TDM works by dividing up time into slices, based on multiples of 16 microsecond ticks. The time slicing is designed to give a maximum dwell time on any frequency of 0.4s (this is to meet US regulations). The TDM algorithm then works as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:707
#: 2f919b6390cb41a9b951f77ba7e1b9f0
msgid "the EEPROM parameters determine a set of TDM parameters, particularly the transmit window and silence period, both are in 16 microsecond units. You can view the results using ATI6."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:710
#: 467918c884f6489682c31566b893c35d
msgid "the transmit window is scaled to allow for 3 full sized packets to be transmitted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:712
#: b7fa228efe4d4a97ad68af35d4eb1317
msgid "the silence period is equal to twice the packet latency, for the given data rate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:714
#: dd9fdc1779084844a3305ee43f224091
msgid "The two radios synchronise their clocks automatically by adding 13 bits of timestamp information to all packets. The timestamp is in 16 microsecond units."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:717
#: d3db2e3307054f368bd5a189f44e9d4d
msgid "Each radio only transmits when it is 'their turn'. So a radio gets one transmit window worth of time, then there is a silence period when neither radio transmits, then the other radio gets its turn. We never have the situation where both radios transmit at the same time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:721
#: 4d4a2ab1612e4b3abed090afe713ade1
msgid "the transmit channels are organised into a random sequence based on the ``NETID``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:723
#: 4d7f1a499dd34c1784d692db20c896e2
msgid "the frequency is changed to the next channel twice for each full TDM round, during the silence periods"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:725
#: a3ae0faa83914141abfa6d5389c893f3
msgid "when not transmitting, data that comes in over the serial port is buffered in a 2048 byte buffer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:727
#: 0c62a8ede5104b29bc832b6325261685
msgid "to prevent the buffer from getting too much data (which increases latency and risks overflow) the radios send information on how full the buffer is to the connected device. ArduPilot adapts its telemetry rates by small amounts to keep the amount of buffered data reasonable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:732
#: a8f70a97bccf442f97c0e505bba90c27
msgid "The TDM algorithm is also adaptive, in the sense that when it is the turn of radio A to transmit, it can send a small token to radio B saying \"I don't need to send anything right now, you can take the rest of my timeslice\". That is how the link auto-balances for asymmetric loads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:737
#: 3cbc32bab6d84733a79907371949a984
msgid "during the initial search for another radio, and any time the link is lost, the radios go into a mode where they move the receiving frequency very slowly but move the transmit frequency at the normal rate. This allows the two radios to find each other for initial clock sync. How long this takes depends on the number of channels, the air data rate and the packet loss rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:744
#: 8cdb228874d247abb6efc35db380bae3
msgid "In some regions you may need to know the distribution of radiated energy within each channel. That depends on a number of factors, but mostly the frequency deviation used for the GFSK modulation. The following formula will give you an estimate of the frequency deviation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:753
#: 6593368211ca49e589e08e39474b906a
msgid "where frequency_deviation is in kHz and the air_data_rate is in kilo bits per second."
msgstr ""
